{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"alias":"crashing-definable-groundhog","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nrebar's store auto-push scrubs ambient `GIT_CONFIG_*` from the child git environment, but supplies no credential source of its own. In any environment where the git credential is carried by ambient config rather than by a config file or a helper on disk, the scrub removes the only way to authenticate and **every** auto-push fails with:\n\n```\nfatal: unable to get password from user\n```\n\nThe write itself succeeds locally, so no data is lost, but the tickets branch silently stops publishing. Each failure also records a `push-pending` sentinel, and that sentinel then wedges every plan-review and completion gate in the clone (that downstream consequence is `abnormal-dear-shoveler` / `16c9-a42d-fd01-48b9`; this ticket is the upstream cause, and the two are separate defects).\n\n## Reproduction\n\nObserved four times in one session, on every tracker write attempted:\n\n1. In a harness that injects git config via `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT` / `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_*` / `GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_*` rather than via `.gitconfig`, run any rebar tracker write, e.g. `rebar comment <id> \"text\"`.\n2. Observe: `WARNING rebar._store.push_classify: tickets branch push failed (exit 128): fatal: unable to get password from user`.\n3. `git -C .tickets-tracker rev-list --count origin/tickets..HEAD` returns a nonzero count that keeps growing across writes.\n4. A manual push from the same tracker directory, with the credential restored, succeeds immediately against the same remote and the same ref.\n\nStep 4 is the discriminator: the credential is valid and the remote is reachable. Only the scrubbed child environment cannot see it.\n\n## Root cause, measured\n\nThe ambient environment carries `credential.interactive = never` and an empty `credential.helper`, plus `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, delivered through `GIT_CONFIG_*` rather than a config file. rebar's push path scrubs `GIT_CONFIG_*` for hermeticity. That scrub is correct in intent - it stops ambient config from perturbing store writes - but it is indiscriminate: it also drops the credential configuration, and rebar does not reinstate any substitute. The child git process is then left with no credential helper, no askpass, and no terminal, so it fails rather than authenticating.\n\nEliminations performed, each measured rather than assumed:\n\n- `GIT_ASKPASS` is **ignored** on this path. The askpass script was verified to work standalone, returning the expected secret.\n- A keychain probe returns empty, and `git credential approve` completes without error yet stores nothing retrievable.\n- Clearing `credential.helper` alone does not reproduce or repair the condition; the interactive prohibition participates.\n\nThe working manual override needs all three of an askpass, `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=1`, and explicit `-c credential.interactive=always -c credential.helper=` - which is precisely the set the scrub removes.\n\n## Why this matters beyond one harness\n\nThe scrub is unconditional, so this is not specific to one vendor's agent harness. Any environment that supplies credentials through ambient git config - CI runners that export `GIT_CONFIG_*`, container entrypoints, sandboxes - will hit the same wall. The failure is silent at the point of use: the rebar command reports success for the write and emits only a warning for the push, so an operator or agent reasonably concludes the store is published when it is not. Divergence accumulates until something downstream refuses.\n\n`scripts/gerrit_safe_submit.py` fails in this environment for the same underlying reason, since it shells out to `git credential fill`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The store push path is able to authenticate in an environment whose git credential configuration arrives exclusively through ambient `GIT_CONFIG_*`, without the operator performing a manual push.\n- [ ] The hermeticity property the scrub exists to protect is preserved: ambient config still cannot perturb store-write behaviour. A test demonstrates both halves - a perturbing ambient key is still ignored, and credential configuration still reaches the push.\n- [ ] A push that fails for lack of a credential is reported distinguishably from a push that fails for an unreachable remote or a rejected ref, so the condition is diagnosable from the message alone.\n- [ ] A regression test reproduces the original failure - it must fail against the pre-fix code path and pass after.\n- [ ] `scripts/gerrit_safe_submit.py` either works under the same conditions or fails with a message that names the missing credential rather than a raw git error.\n- [ ] No new mechanism is introduced without an in-tree justification marker at the definition site, per the mechanism-delta ratchet.","id":"48bd-1c15-c3b0-4a99","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Store auto-push scrubs ambient GIT_CONFIG_*, leaving no credential source: every tickets-branch push fails silently"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789722061979869001,"uuid":"d505208a-ef04-46a3-95ec-06a9cfee7aaf"}